prompt.setup:
// Lock conflict matrix (simplified): ACCESS EXCLUSIVE conflicts with everything;
// ACCESS SHARE conflicts only with ACCESS EXCLUSIVE.
function conflicts(a, b) {
  return a === 'AE' || b === 'AE';
}

// Requests in arrival order: [id, mode, requestedAt, holdDuration]
const requests = [
  ['reader', 'AS', 0, 60],
  ['alter', 'AE', 10, 0],
  ['sel-a', 'AS', 11, 0],
  ['sel-b', 'AS', 12, 0],
  ['sel-c', 'AS', 13, 0],
];

// FIFO grant simulation. A request is granted at max(requestedAt, time the
// queue ahead of it clears for its mode). Because the ALTER (AE) conflicts with
// the held reader and sits ahead of the later SELECTs, nothing after it can be
// granted until the reader releases at t=60.
const held = []; // {mode, releaseAt}
function earliestGrant(mode, notBefore) {
  // can grant once no held lock conflicts and we're past notBefore
  let t = notBefore;
  const blocker = held.find(h => conflicts(mode, h.mode) && h.releaseAt > t);
  if (blocker) t = blocker.releaseAt;
  return t;
}

let queueClearedAt = 0; // FIFO: a request can't be granted before the previous one in line
for (const [id, mode, reqAt, dur] of requests) {
  const grantAt = Math.max(earliestGrant(mode, reqAt), queueClearedAt);
  held.push({ mode, releaseAt: grantAt + dur });
  // FIFO head-of-line: later requests can't jump the queue past this one
  queueClearedAt = grantAt;
  console.log(id + ' GRANTED at t=' + grantAt);
}

model.en:
Set bounds at the top of the session, then run the DDL:
```sql
SET lock_timeout = '2s';
SET statement_timeout = '30s';
ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN status TEXT DEFAULT 'active';
```
With `lock_timeout='2s'`, if the ALTER can't acquire ACCESS EXCLUSIVE within 2 s it aborts with a `lock_not_available` (55P03) error and releases its queue position — so waiting SELECTs are granted within 2 s instead of 60+. The runner catches the error and retries with exponential backoff (1s, 2s, 4s, 8s, 16s), ideally scheduled into a low-traffic window. `statement_timeout` separately caps how long the statement runs once it holds the lock, so a runaway backfill can't pin ACCESS EXCLUSIVE indefinitely. Killing an already-blocking long query (`pg_terminate_backend`) is the manual escape hatch when a retry keeps timing out.

model.ru:
Задай границы в начале сессии, затем выполни DDL:
```sql
SET lock_timeout = '2s';
SET statement_timeout = '30s';
ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN status TEXT DEFAULT 'active';
```
С `lock_timeout='2s'`, если ALTER не смог захватить ACCESS EXCLUSIVE за 2 с, он прерывается с ошибкой `lock_not_available` (55P03) и освобождает место в очереди — поэтому ожидающие SELECT получают блокировку за 2 с вместо 60+. Раннер ловит ошибку и повторяет с экспоненциальным backoff (1s, 2s, 4s, 8s, 16s), желательно в окне низкого трафика. `statement_timeout` отдельно ограничивает, сколько запрос работает, уже держа блокировку, чтобы сбежавший backfill не приколол ACCESS EXCLUSIVE навсегда. Убийство уже блокирующего долгого запроса (`pg_terminate_backend`) — ручной аварийный выход, когда повтор продолжает упираться в таймаут.
